These four files are formulas written by the release tool's own formula renderer (tools/channels/formula.zig in komira-ai/mopm,
the `channels formula` command), not by hand. The checksums are the placeholder ones of that repository's test fixture.

Built on a remote build farm from the repository's `//:channels` target, then run there as one Buck2 action per file:

  channels formula --sums tests/channels/fixtures/SHA256SUMS --name mopm --version 1.2.3 \
    --desc "Package manager for Mojo" --homepage https://mopm.dev --license Apache-2.0 --out <file> \
    <flags below>

mopm.rb                       --repo komira-ai/mopm
mopm_no_version.rb            --repo komira-ai/mopm --no-explicit-version
mopm_file_url.rb              --url-base file:///tmp/dist/
   these three: komira-ai/mopm pull request 142 (drop the Intel Mac release), head bb32d4c31371c2f03400c46cda1db74a3ea5cf7d,
   whose SHA256SUMS fixture lists three tarballs. mopm.rb is byte-identical to that commit's tests/channels/golden/mopm.rb.
mopm_with_macos_intel.rb      --repo komira-ai/mopm
   the renderer on komira-ai/mopm main at 8546b5c03c8077590defc140a73785ff8627f2d4 (four tarballs, with macos-x86_64);
   byte-identical to that commit's golden. The guard must refuse it: Intel macOS is no longer a release platform.

Re-render these when the renderer changes; the guard tests read them as data.
